---
/**
 * The mark: a surveyed summit.
 *
 * WHY THIS
 * Contour lines closing around a high point, with a survey station triangle
 * set on it. Two claims in one shape — this is the high ground (Garrett County
 * holds Maryland's highest land), and it has been measured by someone
 * accountable for the measurement. The second is what the whole site is about.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED AND WHY
 * The first version was a plain benchmark disc: rim, brass face, triangle. The
 * concept was right but the drawing wasn't — a filled triangle inside a brown
 * circle reads as a generic camping or tent glyph at nav size, which is the
 * only size most people ever see it. Contours fix that: nobody else's mark has
 * them, they tie the logo to the topographic sheet the rest of the site is
 * built from, and the irregular closed curves are legible as *terrain* even
 * when they're 20 pixels wide.
 *
 * WHY NOT A WATERFALL OR A LAKE
 * Swallow Falls is one section and Deep Creek Lake is one feature. A mark tied
 * to either dates the brand the moment the next section lands. Terrain and
 * measurement don't go out of date.
 *
 * DRAWING NOTES
 *
 * Stroke widths are absolute against a 100-unit box, so the rendered width is
 * (stroke / 100) x displayed size. That arithmetic is the whole problem with
 * the full mark at small sizes, and the note that used to sit here — "checked
 * at 220/72/40/22px, the innermost contour and the triangle survive at 22" —
 * was wrong. At 22px the inner contour renders 0.79px and EVERY stroke in the
 * mark is sub-pixel. The browser fades them rather than drawing them, so what
 * you actually get is a dark disc with a pale smudge.
 *
 * Worse, 22px was never a size this is used at. It renders at 15.2px in the
 * home page eyebrow, 17.6px in the footer and 33.6px in the masthead. Two of
 * those three are below the point where any of it resolves.
 *
 * Hence `compact`. Below about 24px it drops the two outer contours and the
 * light waterline — detail that is only noise at that scale — and thickens
 * what is left so it clears a pixel. Same silhouette, same idea, fewer lines.
 * This is what the favicon uses too.
 */
interface Props {
  class?: string;
  /** Draw the contours in on first paint. Off inside dense nav rows. */
  animate?: boolean;
  /** For anything under ~24px. Fewer lines, thicker, so it still reads. */
  compact?: boolean;
}
const { class: className = "", animate = false, compact = false } = Astro.props;
---

<svg
  class={`${className} ${animate ? "logo-draw" : ""}`}
  viewBox="0 0 100 100"
  role="img"
  aria-label="Deep Creek Field Guide"
>
  <!-- The disc: a benchmark is set into rock, so the mark sits in something. -->
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49" fill="var(--color-slate)" />

  <g fill="none" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round">
    {/* The two outer contours carry texture, not meaning. They are the first
        thing to go when there aren't enough pixels to draw them. */}
    {!compact && (
      <>
        <path
          d="M14 62c4-11 9-17 17-22s14-13 24-13 17 7 21 14 9 12 9 21"
          stroke="var(--color-contour)"
          stroke-width="3"
          opacity="0.55"
        />
        <path
          d="M23 63c3-9 8-14 14-18s12-10 18-9 13 7 16 13 6 10 6 17"
          stroke="var(--color-contour)"
          stroke-width="3"
          opacity="0.8"
        />
      </>
    )}
    {/* Innermost contour: the line that has to survive whatever the size. */}
    <path
      d="M33 64c2-7 6-11 10-14s8-7 12-6 9 6 11 10 4 7 4 12"
      stroke="var(--color-paper)"
      stroke-width={compact ? 7 : 3.6}
    />
  </g>

  {/* Survey station triangle on the summit — the element that says measured.
      Grown in compact, because at 16px the full-size one is under 3px wide. */}
  {compact
    ? <path d="M50 20 L62 41 L38 41 Z" fill="var(--color-paper)" />
    : <path d="M50 26 L59 43 L41 43 Z" fill="var(--color-paper)" />}
  {!compact && <circle cx="50" cy="38.5" r="2.6" fill="var(--color-slate)" />}

  {/* Waterline: the lake the county is named for. One band in compact — two
      thin ones at 16px are a single grey blur. */}
  <path d="M11 72h78" stroke="var(--color-lake)" stroke-width={compact ? 9 : 4.5} stroke-linecap="round" />
  {!compact && (
    <path d="M20 80h60" stroke="var(--color-lake)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" opacity="0.6" />
  )}
</svg>
